codex
Implemented a native Track‑B tritangent generator based on the paper’s singular-quadric construction and Algorithm 3.1’s square-sextic criterion. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/html/1805.11702v1)

Deliverables:

- [Generator](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1805.11702/gen_1805_11702.py:1)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1805.11702/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1805.11702/README.md)
- [Bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1805.11702/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Structural-hint transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1805.11702/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1805.11702/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Local verification results:

- All G1–G9(c) checks pass.
- Random guessing: 0/200,000.
- Rigorous density upper bound: \(120/503^3 \approx 9.43\times10^{-7}\).
- Five attacks: 0/8 successes each.
- Reference tangent-incidence algorithm: 8/8, up to 3,714,246 operations.
- Compact second-polar route: 8/8, 165 operations.
- Answer size: four field elements, 15 characters in the measured instance.

The result is not ship-ready because OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” for every bare, hinted, and placebo redraw. Zero oracle attempts were counted, so I did not manufacture a STEP 4 hardness verdict. Restoring OpenRouter capacity and rerunning `harden.py` is the remaining blocker.
