-## What the problem is
+## Problem and trust model
 
-The module is based on Celik, Kulkarni, Ren, and Sayyary Namin,
-[“Tritangents and Their Space Sextics”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.11702).
-It gives the solver a rank-three quadric `Q`, a cubic `G`, and an exact weighted-plane
-parametrization of the smooth space sextic `C=V(Q,G)` over `F_p`. The solver must
-return a projective plane and a squarefree binary cubic whose square is the exact
-restriction of `G` to that plane. Verification is only finite-field matrix
-multiplication, substitution, a cubic discriminant, and coefficient comparison.
+This module is based on Celik, Kulkarni, Ren, and Sayyary Namin,
+[“Tritangents and Their Space Sextics”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.11702). It gives
+the solver a singular quadric `Q`, a cubic `F`, and an exact parametrization of
+their smooth degree-six complete intersection over `GF(p)`. The requested witness
+is a normalized linear form defining a plane whose restriction to the curve is
+three distinct double points.
 
-This matches Definition 2.2 and Sections 4.1–4.2 of the paper. Proposition 4.3 is
-important: a plane through the cone vertex is excluded because it represents the
-vanishing even theta characteristic, not one of the odd tritangents sought here.
+Verification never reads `inst["answer"]`. It pulls the proposed plane back through
+the supplied cone parametrization, eliminates the weighted coordinate, and checks
+by exact finite-field polynomial gcds that the resulting binary sextic is a
+nonzero scalar times the square of a squarefree cubic. This is the square-sextic
+criterion used in Algorithm 3.1. Definition 2.2 supplies the tritangent notion;
+Equation (*) in Section 4.1 supplies the weighted-cubic model; Proposition 4.3
+explains why planes through the cone vertex are excluded.
 
 ## Why Track B
 
-Track A would be false. Algorithm 3.1 constructs and solves the tritangent ideal;
-its square locus alone is defined by 45 quartics, and the authors warn that radical
-decomposition is often the costly step. The exact finite-field reference used here
-scans the `p^3` planes outside the vertex hyperplane and square-tests every
-restriction. At the hard preset, eight successful searches tested 462,306 planes in
-8.076783 seconds; that is at most 73,968,960 scalar field operations.
+Track A would be false. Algorithm 3.1 computes all tritangents by resultants and
+the 45 quartic equations defining square binary sextics, and Section 4.2 gives a
+specialized route when the eight blown-up points are known. For this finite-field
+distribution, the executable reference algorithm enumerates `Q(F_p)` through the
+given parametrization and accumulates tangent-plane pencils in `O(p^2)` field
+operations. It solved 8/8 hard instances, with a worst measured 3,714,246 counted
+operations and 0.582 seconds in the final audit run.
 
-The compact route is different. The kernel of the rank-three quadratic form gives
-the cone vertex `v`; on this constructed distribution the second polar `D_v^2 G` is
-the planted plane. The supplied conic chart then recovers the binary-cubic square
-root triangularly. The complete route is bounded by 238 exact field operations. A
-solver has to notice the polar invariant; brute coefficient work does not compress
-to something writable by hand.
+The compact route observes that the second polar of `F` in the cone-vertex
+direction is the trace-zero plane. It recovered the answer on 8/8 instances in
+at most 165 counted field operations. The construction makes this exact: before
+masking,
+
+`F = u^3 + A(s,t)u + (s*t*(s-t))^2 (mod Q)`.
 
-The easy regimes were deliberately avoided. Section 4.2 computes tritangents much
-more directly when the eight blown-up plane points are supplied, so those points
-are not part of the public instance. Planes through the vertex are also mechanically
-tangent but are the even-theta case excluded by Proposition 4.3.
+Thus `u=0` is a tritangent. A squarefree discriminant
+`-4*A^3-27*(s*t*(s-t))^4` certifies smoothness, and a dense `GL(4)` change carries
+the witness to the displayed coordinates. The eight points of Section 4.2 are
+deliberately not supplied, since that is an easy regime identified by the paper.
 
