605:The fact that the Steiner system of $C$ can be constructed purely combinatorially is rooted in a classical connection between theta characteristics of $C$ and quadratic forms over $\JC[2]$ \cite[Section 5]{Dol2012}:
609:can be regarded as a symplectic vector space over $\FF_2$ of dimension $8$. The space of quadratic forms of $\JC[2]$, which we denote by $\mathcal{Q}(\JC[2])$, is a homogeneous space of $\JC[2]$. The disjoint union $\JC[2] \coprod \mathcal{Q}(\JC[2])$ has the natural structure of an $\FF_2$-vector space of dimension $9$. Each quadratic form corresponds to a theta characteristic of $C$.
611:Let $\res_C\colon \Pic(X) \rightarrow \Pic(C)$ be the natural restriction and set $v_i:=\res_C(e_i+\kappa_X)$, where $e_1,\ldots,e_8$ are the eight exceptional divisors of $X$ corresponding to the eight points $P_1,\ldots,P_8$ under the blow-down map. We may construct an Aronhold basis $B$ of $\JC[2] \coprod \mathcal{Q}(\JC[2])$ from the set $\{ v_1, \ldots, v_8, \res_C(-\kappa_X) \}\subseteq\Pic(C)$ via \cite[Theorem II A1.1.]{RauFar1974}. The labeling of a tritangent plane stated above can be understood in terms of the $B$-coordinates of the corresponding odd theta characteristic, viewed as an element of $\JC[2] \coprod \mathcal{Q}(\JC[2])$. For further details, see \cite[Section 1.1.4]{Tur2018}.
613:% Under this correspondence, the eight exceptional divisors on $X$, together with its anticanonical divisor, give rise to a special basis of $\JC[2] \coprod \mathcal{Q}(\JC[2])$ called an Aronhold basis \cite[Theorem II A1.1.]{RauFar1974}. The labeling of the tritangents defined above come from linear expressions of the corresponding quadratic forms in terms of the Aronhold basis.
616:%The $256$ points in $\JC[2]$ together with the Weyl-pairing $\langle \cdot,\cdot\rangle$ can be regarded as a symplectic vector space over $\FF_2$ of dimension $8$. The quadratic forms of $\JC[2]$, which we denote by $\mathcal{Q}(\JC[2])$, is a homogeneous space of $\JC[2]$. The disjoint union $\JC[2] \coprod \mathcal{Q}(\JC[2])$ has the natural structure of an $\FF_2$-vector space of dimension $9$. Each quadratic form corresponds to a theta characteristic of $C$.
617:%Under this correspondence, the eight exceptional divisors on $X$, together with its anticanonical divisor, give rise to a special basis of $\JC[2] \coprod \mathcal{Q}(\JC[2])$ called an Aronhold basis \cite[Theorem II A1.1.]{RauFar1974}. The labeling of the tritangents defined above come from linear expressions of the corresponding quadratic forms in terms of the Aronhold basis.
621:% In general, the theta characteristics of a curve $C$ of genus $g$ are labelled via combinatorial data derived from an Aronhold basis \cite{GroHar2001}. We can recover an Aronhold basis from a fundamental set in $\JC[2]$, as described in \cite[Theorem II A1.1.]{RauFar1974}.
